# Loop Analysis

An automated insulin delivery system (Loop, AAPS, Trio, iAPS or OpenAPS) makes
small changes to your insulin all day. **Loop Analysis** shows these changes,
when they occurred, and what occurred after them. Use it to find answers to
questions such as these:

- _"Does my loop always add insulin at 3 in the morning?"_
- _"What does my glucose do after breakfast?"_
- _"How frequently does the loop not operate?"_

To open it, go to **Reports > Loop Analysis**. Anyone whose access includes
reports can open it. This includes members, guest links and apps that you
authorise. A public share link shows the pages, but it cannot show the figures,
because the share does not get the time zone.

<Callout type="warning" title="Observations, not advice">

  All of Loop Analysis is an observation of your own data. Each figure shows the
  number of days or events that it uses. No figure is an instruction. Speak to
  your diabetes care team before you change a setting.

</Callout>

## The five pages

Loop Analysis has five pages. The page switcher under the report header links
the pages. When you go to a different page, the range and the filters stay the
same.

| Page | The question that it answers |
|------|------------------------------|
| [Daily profile](/docs/reports/loop-analysis/daily-profile) | What does a usual day look like, hour by hour? When does the loop add or remove insulin? |
| [Meals and events](/docs/reports/loop-analysis/meals-and-events) | What occurs after a meal, a correction, a rise with no carbs logged, or the start of exercise or a new sensor? |
| [Prediction accuracy](/docs/reports/loop-analysis/prediction-accuracy) | How close are the predictions of the loop to the readings that followed them? |
| [Loop health](/docs/reports/loop-analysis/loop-health) | How much of the time was the loop closed, and how frequently did it reach its limits? |
| [Settings evidence](/docs/reports/loop-analysis/settings-evidence) | What does your data show about your basal rates, insulin sensitivity (ISF) and carb ratio? |

## The Filters panel

To change the range and the filters, open the **Filters** panel of the reports.
The Loop Analysis filters are under the date range.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-filter-bar" />

- The date range: the days that the report uses. The maximum is 90 days. For a
  longer range, the report uses only the most recent 90 days, and it tells you
  this.
- **Compare with**: a second period to show with the first. Select
  **Previous period** for the period immediately before, or **Custom range** to
  select the dates. The charts show the comparison as dashed outlines. The Loop
  health tiles and the chart readouts show the compared value.
- **Profile**: use only the time when one profile was active.
- **Days of the week**: use only some days of the week, for example weekdays.
- **Exclude the time of**: remove the time of overrides, temporary targets,
  exercise, illness, or time not in closed loop. Illness and time not in closed
  loop are removed by default. The report removes only that time. It does not
  remove the full day.

**Time not in closed loop** uses the dates when each of your pumps was in use.
These dates are days in your time zone, from local midnight to local midnight.

The report always removes glucose readings that your data marks as not
reliable. Examples are compression lows (a false low when you lie on your
sensor) and sensor errors.

### Ranges that include today

A range never goes after the current time. If the range includes today, the
report uses today only up to now. When you compare with the previous period,
the comparison also stops at the same time of day on its last day.

### Time zone

Loop Analysis aligns days by the local time of day. It needs the time zone of
your data to do this. If it cannot get the time zone, each page shows
**This report needs a time zone** and the cause:

- **The profile settings do not have a time zone.** The owner of this data can
  set a time zone in the profile settings.
- **This server does not recognise the time zone in the profile settings.** The
  owner of this data can correct the time zone in the profile settings.
- **The time zone of this data did not load.** To try again, reload the report
  later.
- **A shared link does not show the time zone of this data.**

## Net loop correction

Many figures use **net loop correction**. This is the insulin that your loop
added above your scheduled basal, or removed from it. It is the sum of two
decisions of the algorithm:

- **Temp basals that the algorithm set**, measured against your scheduled
  basal. The schedule is adjusted for an override that changes your insulin
  needs. A temp above the schedule adds insulin. A temp below the schedule
  (also a zero temp) removes insulin.
- **Automatic boluses** (SMBs in AAPS, Trio, iAPS and OpenAPS, or the automatic
  boluses of Loop).

Many systems make most of their corrections with automatic boluses. So the
figure includes them. If it did not include them, it would show only a part of
what the loop does.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-net-correction" />

Some insulin changes are not decisions of the algorithm. Net loop correction
does not include them. The Daily profile can show them as separate lines:

- temp basals that you set manually;
- pump suspends;
- delivery that the report calculated from the schedule (**Inferred**).

### Which temp basals the loop set

Trio, AAPS and OpenAPS do not record whether a temp basal came from the loop or
from you. Nocturne compares each temp with the decisions that your loop uploaded
near it:

- If the loop asked for the same rate within 5 minutes, the temp is from the
  algorithm.
- If the loop asked only for different rates, the temp is manual.
- If the loop uploaded no decisions near the temp, the temp keeps the origin
  that its uploader gives. This is the algorithm for a loop app, and manual for
  other sources.

The temp and the decision can come from devices with different names. For
example, AAPS can upload through Nightscout with no device name, and Trio
through Tidepool. Nocturne then uses the decisions of the one loop device that
uploaded near the temp. If two or more loop devices uploaded near the temp, the
temp keeps the origin that its uploader gives.

### Delivered basal

The report calculates delivered basal from the temp basal records of the pump.

- **Two sources for one pump.** Two sources can record the same pump, for
  example a loop and a Tidepool copy of it. The records of the loop are used
  first. The records of the other source fill only the time that the loop did
  not record.
- **A cancelled temp.** When a temp is cancelled, basal goes back to the
  schedule. The report uses the schedule for this time only when the rule for
  missing records applies.
- **Missing records.** If there is no record of delivery, the basal for that
  time is not known. The report uses the schedule only for a gap of 3 hours or
  less, with records on the two sides. A longer gap stays unknown.

## Read the charts

Most charts in Loop Analysis show a shaded band, not a single line.

- The **line** in the middle is the median. Half of your days (or readings) were
  above it and half were below it.
- The **darker band** shows the middle half (25th to 75th percentile).
- The **lighter band** shows most of your data (10th to 90th percentile).
- **n** is the number of days, readings or events that a figure uses.

The report measures two types of figure differently:

- For **glucose**, the report puts all the readings from all the days into each
  five-minute period. The AGP report does the same, so the bands agree with AGP.
  For glucose, n counts readings.
- **Rates** (basal, net loop correction, insulin on board and carbs on board)
  are calculated for each day first. Then the report compares the days. For
  rates, n counts days. A day counts in a period only when data covers half of the
  period or more.

If a figure uses **fewer than 5 days**, the chart shows it faded. There is not
enough data to show a pattern.

The report shows missing data as missing, never as zero. A zero temp is a real
zero, and it always counts.

### When parts are hidden

Your access can include only some types of data. Then some parts of the report
show **Hidden: your access does not include this data**. Each part needs access
to the type of data that it uses: glucose, treatments (insulin and carbs),
devices (the records of the loop and the pump) or therapy settings (your
profile). Filters that you cannot use are grey, and they show the access that
they need.

If you can see only the **last 24 hours**, the report shows only those 24 hours
for all ranges. It does not compare ranges. Settings evidence does not show. The
report tells you this.

## How it is different from the Nightscout Loopalyzer

Loop Analysis replaces the Loopalyzer report of Nightscout. It corrects the
problems that made Loopalyzer difficult to trust:

- **Zeros count.** Loopalyzer did not use zeros in its averages. So the loop
  correction looked larger than it was.
- **Automatic boluses count.** Loopalyzer showed only temp basals. If your loop
  corrects with SMBs, Loopalyzer showed only a part of what it did.
- **Daylight saving time is correct.** Loopalyzer did not use days that were not
  exactly 24 hours long. After a change, it showed glucose with an error of one
  hour.
- **Readings exactly on a five-minute mark stay in.** One missing basal record
  does not move all the times after it.
- **The spread and n are shown**, not only an average.
- **Totals**: insulin each day, with basal, automatic boluses and manual boluses.
- **A maximum of 90 days**, not 14.
- **Meals align correctly.** Loopalyzer moved the time from the first meal in the
  data, not from the first meal in the day. It also lost data that moved past
  midnight.
- **You can remove time**: overrides, temporary targets, exercise, illness and
  time not in closed loop.
- **One method for Loop, AAPS, Trio, iAPS and OpenAPS.**
